From 577811071583aa0943000d5119bd30c3a1fbbcc8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>

Date: Wed, 6 Aug 2025 12:14:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] enable plan_cache, a query incluing a scalar
 subquery in select filed, and in the where clause contains a condition
 judgment as length(BLOB)<?, the query will return a wrong result set after
 using cached plan for several times.

This bug report contains two different problems. One of wrong
rows (fixed here), one of lost order (fixed in a next commit).

The query has WHERE and HAVING.

first execution:
- the WHERE is duplicated (copy_andor_structure); it is a 2-argument
AND
- it ends up into QEP_TAB::condition()
- plan is cached: duplicates QEP_TAB::condition() (let's name the
resulting condition (Item*) as COND_CACHE)
- JOIN::add_having_as_tmp_table_cond() changes QEP_TAB::condition():
it adds the HAVING condition to it, which (as a side effect of
fix_fields on the new AND) empties the old AND Item
(look for 'clear()' in the patch). QEP_TAB::condition() is now a
3-arg AND in a different Item_cond_and. COND_CACHE is still
non-empty.

second execution:
- cached plan is applied: QEP_TAB::condition() is set to be
COND_CACHE (AND of 2 args)
- JOIN::add_having_as_tmp_table_cond changes QEP_TAB::condition(), so
empties COND_CACHE

third execution:
- cached plan is applied: QEP_TAB::condition() is set to be COND_CACHE (empty)
- so one part of conditions got lost.

This is a bug specific of the 3rd execution.

Fix: due to the fact that, even when using a cached plan, some
destructive transformations are done by parts of the
optimizer (JOIN::add_having_as_tmp_table_cond() here): when applying
the cached plan, do copy_andor_structure() to preserve COND_CACHE for
next executions.
